==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|dette|det||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʰeh₁bʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|dette}}, {{m|enm|dett}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|dete}} ({{cog|fr|dette}}), from {{der|en|la-med|dēbita}}, from {{der|en|la|dēbitum||what is owed, a debt, a duty}}, neuter of {{m|la|dēbitus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|dēbeō||to owe}}, contraction of {{m|la|*dehibeō||I have from}}, from {{m|la|de||from}} + {{m|la|habeō||to have}}. {{doublet|en|debit}}. Displaced native {{noncog|ang|eofot}}.

The unpronounced "b" in the modern English spelling is a [[Latinisation]] from the Latin etymon {{m|la|dēbitum}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛt/|[ˈdɛt]}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-debt.ogg|a=US}}
* {{a|en|[[spelling pronunciation]]}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛbt/|/ˈdɛp(t)/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛt|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|debt}}
* {{hmp|en|death|aa=th-stopping}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An action, state of mind, or object one has an [[obligation]] to [[perform]] for another, [[adopt]] toward another, or [[give]] to another.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1589|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=Henry IV, Part I|section=act 1, scene 3|passage=Revenge the jeering and disdain'd contempt / Of this proud king, who studies day and night / To answer all the '''debt''' he owes to you / Even with the bloody payment of your deaths.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1850|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|title=w:The Scarlet Letter|chapter=14|passage=This long '''debt''' of confidence, due from me to him, whose bane and ruin I have been, shall at length be paid.}}
# The state or condition of owing something to another.
#: {{ux|en|I am in your '''debt'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We owe them a '''debt''' of gratitude for taking care of our pets.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lucrece|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SjYOAQAAMAAJ&pg=PT38|passage=The petty ſtreames that paie a dailie '''det''' / To their ſalt ſoveraigne with their freſh [[falls|fals]] [[haste|haſt]], / Adde to his flowe, but alter not his [[tast|taſt]].}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author={{w|Kurt Cobain}}|title={{w|Heart-Shaped Box}}|url=https://youtu.be/n6P0SitRwy8?feature=shared|album={{w|In Utero}}|artist={{w|Nirvana (band)|Nirvana}}|year=1993|text=Hey! Wait! I've got a new complaint / Forever in '''debt''' to your priceless advice}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} [[money|Money]] that one person or entity [[owe]]s or is required to [[pay]] to another, generally as a result of a [[loan]] or other [[financial]] transaction.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1919|author=w:Upton Sinclair|title=Jimmie Higgins|chapter=15|passage=Bolsheviki had repudiated the four-billion-dollar '''debt''' which the government of the Tsar had contracted with the bankers.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=Engineers of a different kind|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers. Piling '''debt''' onto companies’ balance-sheets is only a small part of what leveraged buy-outs are about, they insist. Improving the workings of the businesses they take over is just as core to their calling, if not more so. Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170608005531/http://www.economist.com:80/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers|archivedate=8 June 2017}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2004
|author=w:George Carlin
|title=w:When Will Jesus Bring the Pork Chops?
|url=https://archive.org/details/sacredmountainof0000john/
|location=[[New York]]
|publisher=w:Hyperion Books
|ISBN=1-4013-0821-X
|OCLC=757869006
|OL=24604921M
|page=213
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/whenwilljesusbri00hype/page/213/
|text=I don't own any stocks or bonds. All my money is tied up in '''debt'''.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} An [[action]] at law to recover a certain specified sum of money alleged to be due<ref>{{R:Burrill Law Dictionary}}</ref>

