===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|en|to wet with dew}}
From {{inh|en|enm|dewe}}, {{m|enm|dewen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*dēawian}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*dauwēn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*dawwāną}}. Cognates include {{cog|stq|daue}}, {{cog|de|tauen}} and {{cog|nl|dauwen}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[wet]] with, or as if with, dew; to [[moisten]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1887|author=Andrew B. Saxton|chapter=Sunken Graves|title=The Century
|passage=The grasses grew&nbsp;/ A little ranker since they '''dewed''' them so.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To deposit dew.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The American Almanac and Repository of Useful Knowledge for ...|year=1831|page=80|text=The former [substances extremely attentuated, or in a filamentous and downly state] collect dew copiously, while the latter [solids] so rarely indicate its presence, even when it is '''dewing''' freely on contiguous substances, that some observers have doubted whether it ever happened, and have been disposed to believe that they repel it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Six of Them|author=Cecily Sidgwick|year=1929|page=118|text=Yesterday she tied Kenneth to a tree and left him there for hours when it was '''dewing'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Caesar's Last Breath: Decoding the Secrets of the Air Around Us|author=Sam Kean|year=2017|text=Withdraw heat from a gas, and it will '''dew''' into a liquid. Subtract more heat, and a solid forms.}}

