# {{non-gloss|Senses relating to moving from a higher to a lower position.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pass#Verb|pass]] from a [[high#Adjective|higher]] to a [[low#Adjective|lower]] [[part#Noun|part]] of (something, such as a [[flight#Noun|flight]] of [[stair]]s or a [[slope#Noun|slope]]); to [[go down]] along or upon.
##: {{ux|en|they '''descended''' the river in boats}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''descend''' a ladder}}
##: {{ux|en|The noble prince '''descended''' to one knee to ask for the princess’s hand in marriage.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=X|line=1497|page=347|passage=[T]hey both '''deſcend''' the Hill; {{...}}|year=1873}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Sublime and Beautiful|sectionnumber=XVII|section=The Effects of Blackness|page=146|passage=[I]f, after '''deſcending''' a flight of ſtairs, vve attempt inadvertently to take another ſtep in the manner of the former ones, the ſhock is extreamly rude and diſagreeable; and by no art, can vve cauſe ſuch a ſhock by the ſame means, vvhen vve expect and prepare for it.}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Parisina|page=87|stanza=XX|lines=536–537|passage=But never tear his cheek '''descended''', / And never smile his brow unbended; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=II|page=152|passage=I saw him '''descend''' the mountain with greater speed than the flight of an eagle, and quickly lost among the undulations of the sea of ice.}}
##* {{RQ:Priestley English Journey|section=1|page=323|passage=[T]hese men '''descend''' very steep and dangerous cliffs near Seaham Harbour and pick up coal from the shore.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} Of a flight of stairs, a [[road#Noun|road]], etc.: to [[lead#Verb|lead]] [[down#Preposition|down]] (a [[hill#Noun|hill]], a slope, etc.).
##: {{ux|en|They took the steep path that '''descends''' the hill down to the beach.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1957 August|author=H. P. White|title=The Tonbridge-Hastings Line and its Traffic|journal=Railway Magazine|page=528|text=Soon after Crowhurst, the down grade steepens to 1 in 100 as the line '''descends''' the side of a valley, at the mouth of which a glimpse of the sea can be caught—the only one.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[move#Verb|move]] (someone or something) from a higher to a lower [[place#Noun|place]] or [[position#Noun|position]]; to [[bring#Verb|bring]] or [[send#Verb|send]] (someone or something) down.
##* {{RQ:Hale Mankind|chapter=Concerning Vegetables, and Especially ''Insecta Animalia''; whether any of Them are ''Sponte Orta'', or Arise Not rather ''Ex Præexistente Semine''|page=267|passage=[T]he common Devv exhaled from ſome ſorts of Herbs or VVeeds, but eſpecially from the common Graſs, carries vvith it the Seminal Tincture of the Herb, vvhich being again '''deſcended''' by Devvs or Rain upon the bare and naked Earth, re-produceth the ſame ''Species'': {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[physically]] move or pass from a higher to a lower place or position; to [[come#Verb|come]] or [[go#Verb|go]] down in any [[way#Noun|way]], such as by [[climb#Verb|climbing]], [[fall#Verb|falling]], [[flow#Verb|flowing]], [[walk#Verb|walking]], etc.; to move [[downwards]]; to fall, to [[sink#Verb|sink]].
##: {{antonyms|en|ascend|climb|go up|rise}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Sherren Brewer|J[ohn] S[herren] Brewer]], compiler|chapter=[A Commission of Sewers for the Marches of Calais, {{...|to Sir Rob. Wingfield, Deputy of Calais, Sir Chr. Garnysshe, Porter of the Same Town, William H[ussey?], Controller of the Town, Sir Ric. Whetell, Rob. F.&nbsp;……, Sub-treasurer, the Mayor of the Town, [Lieutenant] of the Staple, Hen. Planckney, Massingberd,&nbsp;……, and Th. Prowde.}} Dated Hampton Court, 14 Sept. 20 Hen. VIII.]|title=Letters and papers, Foreign and Domestic, of the Reign of {{w|Henry VIII}}.{{nb...|Preserved in the Public Record Office, the British Museum and Elsewhere in England.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman & Co.]], and [[w:Nicholas Trübner|Trübner & Co.]],{{nb...|Paternoster Row; also by Parker & Co., Oxford; and Macmillan & Co., Cambridge; A. & C. Black, Edinburgh; and A. Thom, Dublin.}}|year='''1528''' September 24 (Gregorian calendar)|year_published=1872|volume=IV, part II|section=paragraph 5102 (Calais)|page=2232|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/letterspaperspt204greauoft/page/2232/mode/1up|oclc=277886749|passage=And there is another watergang, called Haile Fayers watergang, 4 miles long and 16 feet broad, which '''descendeth''' by a spoye of stone at Hofkirk bridge into the said great river, which must always be cast at the tenants' cost.}}
##* {{RQ:T. More Workes|title=Comfort|book=II|chapter=Of the Deuill Named ''Negotium Perambulans in Tenebris'', that is to Wit, Busines Walking in the Darkenesses|page=1124|column=2|passage=They leade theyr life in pleaſure, & at a poppe, down they '''deſcende''' into hell.}}
##* {{RQ:Book of Common Prayer|chapter=An Ordre for Euensong throughout the Yeare|folio=vii|passage=So God and man is one [[w:Jesus|Chriſte]]. / Who ſuffered for oure ſaluacion: '''deſcended''' into hell, roſe agayne the third daye from the dead. / He aſcended into heauen, he ſytteth on the right hand of the father, God almighty: from whence he ſhall come to iudge the quicke and dead.}}
##* {{RQ:Estienne Liebault Maison Rustique|chapter=Langvier|page=32|passage={{...}} ''Noe'' [''i.e.'', {{w|Noah}}] vvith his familie comming out of the arke vpon the top of the mount Cordicus, '''deſcended''' into the plaine at the foote of the mountaine filled vvith dead bodies (vvhich is called ''Miri Adam'', vvhich is as much as the place of bovvelled men) {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Matthew|7|25|page=1227|column=1|passage=And the raine '''deſcended''', and the floods came, and the windes blew, and beat vpon that houſe; and it fell not: for it was founded vpon a rock.}}
##* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|chapter=Apology|page=14|passage=Dark Clouds bring VVaters, vvhen the bright bring none / Yea, dark or bright, if they their Silver drops / Cause to '''deſcend''', the Earth, by yielding crops, / Gives praiſe to both, and carpeth not at either, / But treaſures up the Fruit they yield together: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Herrick Hesperides|poem=Harmar|page=357|passage=But this I knovv, ſhould [[w:Jupiter (god)|Jupiter]] agen / '''Deſcend''' from heaven, to re-converſe vvith men; / The Romane Language full and ſuperfine, / If ''Iove'' vvo'd ſpeake, he vvo'd accept of thine.}}
##* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=II|page=31|passage=The air was cold, and the rain again began to '''descend''': we entered the hut, the fiend with an air of exultation, I with a heavy heart and depressed spirits.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Arthur Hugh Clough|chapter=[Untitled poem]|title=Poems: With a Memoir|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire&#59; London|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Publishers|Macmillan and Co.]]{{nb...|Cambridge and 23 Henrietta Street, Covent Garden, London}}||year=a. '''1862''' (date written)|year_published=1862|page=76|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/poemswithmemo00clou/page/76/mode/1up|oclc=1231115385|passage=O stream '''descending''' to the sea, / Thy mossy banks between, / The flow'rets blow, the grasses grow, / The leafy trees are green. // In garden plots the children play, / The fields the labourers till, / And houses stand on either hand, / And thou '''descendest''' still.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|translator=w:Edward William Lane|title=[[w:One Thousand and One Nights|The Thousand and One Nights, Commonly Called, in England, The Arabian Nights’ Entertainments.{{nb...|A New Translation from the Arabic, with Copious Notes. [...] In Three Volumes.}}]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Charles Knight (publisher)|Charles Knight and Co.]],{{nb...|Ludgate Street.}}|year=1841|volume=III|page=453|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sZIWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA453|oclc=1127756726|passage=And they accosted the sheykh Abu-r-Ruweysh and said to him, O our sheykh, Bahrám practised a strategem to effect his ascent to the top of the mountain, and how did he '''descend''', and what wonders did he see upon the mountain? The sheykh Abu-r-Ruweysh therefore said, O Ḥasan, tell them how thou '''descendedst''', and acquaint them with the wonders that thou sawest.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Alexander Watson|chapter=Whit Sunday. [A Prayer for Whitsuntide.]|title=The Churchman’s Sunday Evenings at Home|location=London|publisher=W. J. Cleaver,{{nb...|Baker Street, Portman Square.}}; Rugeley, Staffordshire: J. T. Walters|year=1844|volume=II|page=163|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=t-6ierwDCq4C&pg=PA163|oclc=57649900|passage=Let that mighty rushing wind, in which aforetime Thou '''descendedst''', purge away the chaff of our carnal affections, and with a holy violence beat down our strongholds of sin, and all the proud imaginations that resist Thy grace.}}
##* {{RQ:Priestley English Journey|section=1|page=330|passage=As it is, the miner is one of the most overworked and deplorably underpaid men in the country. {{...}} I know very well that if your supply of coal depended on my walking several miles to a pithead, '''descending''' in a cage for half a mile, walking again to the dwindling tunnel where I had to work, then slogging away for about seven hours in that hell, all for something like two pounds a week, your grates would be empty.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Griesemer|chapter=20|title=w:No One Thinks of Greenland|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Picador (imprint)|Picador]]|month=May|year=2001|page=187|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/noonethinksofgre00grie/page/187/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-312-27457-3|passage=Rudy felt a gust of fear rise in his chest, and he looked again in the mirror, but the hangar and stable were now beyond the rise, out of sight, he was '''descending''' so fast.}}
### {{lb|en|astrology}} Of a [[zodiac sign]]: to move away from the [[zenith]] towards the [[horizon]]; to [[sink#Verb|sink]]; also, of a [[planet]]: to move to a place where it has less [[astrological]] [[significance]].
### {{lb|en|astronomy}} Of a [[celestial body]]: to move away from the zenith towards the horizon; to sink; also, to move towards the [[south#Noun|south]].
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IV|lines=540–543|page=110|passage=[T]he ſetting Sun / Slowly '''deſcended''', and with right aſpect / Againſt the eaſtern Gate of Paradiſe / Leveld his eevning Rayes: {{...}}|year=1873}}
###* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=III|pages=116–117|pageref=116|passage=The moon had reached her summit in the heavens and was beginning to '''descend'''; the clouds swept across it swifter than the flight of the vulture and dimmed her rays, while the lake reflected the scene of the busy heavens, rendered still busier by the restless waves that were beginning to rise.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:James Hogg|title=Flodden Field|year=a. '''1836''' (date written)<!--year after author's death-->|newversion=republished in|2ndauthor=Robert Murray|chapter2=James Hogg|title2=Hawick Songs and Song Writers|edition2=3rd|location2=Hawick, Roxburghshire|publisher2=W. & J. Kennedy|year2=1897|page2=31|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=wZtHAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA31|oclc2=563108450|passage=Sol, with broaden'd orb, '''descending''', / Left fierce warriors still contending, / Brilliant Vesper shed her glances, / Ere they sheathed their blood-stained lances.}}
### {{lb|en|biology|physiology}} Of a [[body part]]: to move downwards, especially during [[development]] of the [[embryo]]; specifically, of the [[testis|testes]] of a [[mammal]]: to move downwards from the [[abdominal cavity]] into the [[scrotum]].
###: {{ux|en|Cryptorchidism is the failure of one or both testes to '''descend''' into the scrotum.}}
### {{lb|en|chemistry|obsolete}} Of a [[liquid#Adjective|liquid]] [[substance#Noun|substance]]: to [[distil]] out from another substance and [[gather#Verb|gather]] at the [[bottom#Noun|bottom]] of a [[container]]; also, to distil a substance to obtain another liquid substance in this [[manner#Noun|manner]].
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[slope#Verb|slope]] or [[stretch#Verb|stretch]] downwards.
##* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=XVII|lines=230–231|page=118|passage=Novv paſs'd the rugged road, they journey dovvn / The cavern'd vvay '''deſcending''' to the tovvn, {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|chiefly|historical}} To [[alight#Verb|alight]] from a [[carriage]], a [[horse#Noun|horse]], etc.; also, to [[disembark]] from a [[vessel#Noun|vessel]]; to [[land#Verb|land]].
##* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=XV|lines=437–440|page=48|passage=Their ſails they loos'd, they laſh'd the maſt aſide, / And caſt their anchors, and the cables ty'd: / Then on the breezy ſhore '''deſcending''', join / In grateful banquet o'er the roſy vvine.}}
##* {{RQ:Twain Warner Gilded Age|chapter=XVII|page=164|passage=About ten o'clock a horse and wagon was descried making a slow approach to the camp over the prairie. {{...}} When the conveyance at length drew up to Mr. Thompson's door, the gentleman '''descended''' with great deliberation, straightened himself up, rubbed his hands, and beaming satisfaction from every part of his radiant frame, advanced to the group that was gathered to welcome him, and which had saluted him by name as soon as he came within hearing.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}}
### To come or go down, or [[reduce]], in [[intensity]] or some other [[quality#Noun|quality]].
###* {{RQ:Hall Meditations and Vowes|para=78|page=199|passage={{...}} VVinter comes on ſoftly, firſt by colde devves, then hoare froſts, vntill at laſt it '''deſcende''' to the hardeſt vveather of all: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|title=The False One|act=V|scene=ii|page=139|column=1|passage=Thy glories novv have touch'd the higheſt point, / And muſt '''deſcend'''.}}
### Of a [[physical#Adjective|physical]] [[thing]] (such as a [[cloud#Noun|cloud]] or [[storm#Noun|storm]]) or a (generally [[negative#Adjective|negative]]) [[immaterial#Adjective|immaterial]] thing (such as [[darkness]], [[gloom#Noun|gloom]], or [[silence#Noun|silence]]): to [[settle#Verb|settle]] upon and [[start#Verb|start]] to [[affect#Verb|affect]] a [[person#Noun|person]] or place.
###* {{RQ:Garnier Kyd Cornelia|act=I|sig=B|verso=1|page=20|passage=Death dvvels vvithin vs, and if gentle Peace / '''Diſcend''' not ſoone, our ſorrovves to ſurceaſe, / ''Latium'' (alreadie quaild) vvill be deſtroyd.}}
###* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=IV|lines=1009–1012|page=205|passage=Hear, Goddeſs, hear, by thoſe oblations vvon; / And for the pious fire preſerve the ſon: / His vviſh'd return vvith happy povv'r befriend, / And on the Suitors let thy vvrath '''deſcend'''.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=&#91;{{w|Joseph Converse Heywood}}&#93;|title={{w|Salome}}, the Daughter of {{w|Herodias}}. A Dramatic Poem|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:G. P. Putnam's Sons|Putnam]],{{nb...|532 Broadway.}}|year=1862|pages=244–245|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/salomeherodias00heywrich/page/245/mode/1up|oclc=5058560|passage=Death spreadeth darkly above thee, / '''Descendeth''', '''descendeth''' upon thee, / Suffocating! suffocating! ah!}}
###* {{RQ:Twain Warner Gilded Age|chapter=XXV|page=230|passage=And she managed to keep the good news to herself, though it would seem that the most careless observer might have seen by her springing step and her radiant countenance that some fine piece of good fortune had '''descended''' upon her.}}
###* {{RQ:Priestley English Journey|section=4|page=274|passage=[A]ll the tall chimneys in it [a photograph]—and I can count twenty-five—are smoking furiously, belching out thick dark clouds that look as if they will '''descend''' on the ugly conglomeration of roofs below. That is what it must have looked like throughout all the great days of the industry.}}
### In [[speech#Noun|speech]] or [[writing#Noun|writing]]: to [[proceed]] from one [[matter#Noun|matter]] to another; especially, to pass from more [[general#Adjective|general]] or [[important]] to [[specific#Adjective|specific]] or less important matters to be [[consider]]ed.
###* {{RQ:Fuller Waltham Abbey|chapter=The Re-foundation of Waltham-Abby by [[w:Henry II of England|Henry the Second]]|page=11|passage=But this ſubject begins to ſvvell beyond the bounds intended unto it, leſt therefore vvhat vve intended but a ''Tract'' ſhould ſvvell to a ''Tome'', vve vvill here '''deſcend''' to matters of later date.}}
###* {{RQ:Idler|issue=72|page=1|column=3|passage=He imagined that he had novv purchaſed the privilege of diſcourſe and began to '''deſcend''' to familiar queſtions, endeavouring to accommodate his diſcourſe to the groſſneſs of ruſtick underſtandings.}}
###* {{RQ:Burke Regicide Peace|letter=3|page=121|passage=But let us '''deſcend''' to particulars.}}
###* {{RQ:Macaulay Edinburgh Review|volume=I|page=70|passage=Historians rarely '''descend''' to those details from which alone the real state of a community can be collected.}}
### ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[into]]''' ''or'' '''[[to#Preposition|to]]''': of a [[situation]]: to [[become]] [[worse#Adjective|worse]]; to [[decline#Verb|decline]], to [[deteriorate]].
###: {{synonyms|en|degenerate}}
###: {{ux|en|The meeting '''descended''' into chaos.}}
### ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[on#Preposition|on]]''' ''or'' '''[[upon]]''': to [[make#Verb|make]] an [[attack#Noun|attack]] or [[incursion]], from or as if from a [[vantage#Noun|vantage]] [[ground#Noun|ground]]; to come [[suddenly]] and with [[violence#Noun|violence]].
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=Hanns Oertel|title=Article IV. The [[w:Jaiminiya Upanishad Brahmana|Jāiminīya or Talavakāra Upaniṣad Brāhmaṇa]]: Text, Translation, and Notes|journal=w:Journal of the American Oriental Society|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Tuttle, Morehouse & Taylor, printers to {{w|Yale University}} for the {{w|American Oriental Society}}|month=April|year=1893|year_published=1896|volume=XVI|page=201|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/journalofamerica16ameruoft/page/201/mode/1up|issn=0003-0279|oclc=47785421|passage=When moving down against [him] thou '''descendest''' against the sleeping man unknowing, let [[w:Varuna|Varuṇa]] cover me with a golden armor.}}
###* {{RQ:Priestley English Journey|section=1|page=288|passage=I suffer at times from damp snivelling colds in the head, which '''descend''' upon me quite suddenly, turning me into a helpless and disgusting red-nosed and red-eyed animal feebly crying for still more handkerchiefs.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Alfred Grossmith Mason; Julie Grossmith Deltrice|title=Arctic Warriors: A Personal Account of Convoy PQ18|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=v4FsBAAAQBAJ&pg=PT48|location=Barnsley, South Yorkshire|publisher=Pen & Sword Maritime, [[w:Pen and Sword Books|Pen & Sword Books]]|year=2013|isbn=978-1-78303-037-8|passage=[T]he low growl of engines coming from high above the cloud cover, alerts us to the fact that there are many more aircraft '''descending''' on us than had done during previous visits from the snoopers in their usual ones and twos.}}
### ''Chiefly followed by'' '''on''' ''or'' '''upon''': to [[arrive]] [[suddenly]] or [[unexpectedly]], especially in a manner that [[cause#Verb|causes]] [[disruption]] or [[inconvenience#Noun|inconvenience]].
###: {{ux|en|My neighbour '''descended''' upon me just as I was walking out the door.}}
### To [[come down]] to a [[humble#Adjective|humbler]] or less [[fortunate]], or a worse or less [[virtuous]], [[rank#Noun|rank]] or [[state#Noun|state]]; to [[abase]] or [[lower#Verb|lower]] oneself; to [[condescend]] or [[stoop#Verb|stoop]] to something.
###: {{ux|en|He '''descended''' from his high estate.}}
###* {{RQ:Hall Vertues and Vices|chapter=Of the Truly-noble|page=54|passage=[I]f (as ſeldome) he '''deſcend''' to diſports of chance, his games ſhall neuer make him either pale vvith feare, or hote vvith deſire of gaine.}}
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VIII|lines=168–171|page=231|passage=But what will not Ambition and Revenge / '''Deſcend''' to? vvho aſpires muſt down as low / As high he ſoard, obnoxious firſt or laſt / To baſeſt things.|year=1873}}
###* {{RQ:Johnson Rambler|issue=208|date=17 March 1752|page=171|passage=If I have not been diſtinguiſhed by the diſtributers of literary honours, I have ſeldom '''deſcended''' to any of the arts by vvhich favour is obtained.}}
###* {{RQ:Byron Giaour|edition=8th|page=42|lines=850–852|passage=Not oft to smile '''descendeth''' he, / And when he doth 'tis sad to see / That he but mocks at Misery.}}
###* {{RQ:Macaulay Edinburgh Review|volume=I|page=92|passage=But that a shrewd statesman, whose earliest works are characterised by manliness of thought and language, should, at near sixty years of age, '''descend''' to such puerility, is utterly inconceivable.}}
### {{lb|en|chiefly|poetic|or|religion}} ''Chiefly in the form'' '''descend into''' ''(or'' '''[[within#Preposition|within]]''''')'' '''oneself''': to [[mentally]] [[enter#Noun|enter]] a state of ([[deep#Adjective|deep]]) [[meditation]] or [[thought#Noun|thought]]; to [[retire#Verb|retire]].
###* {{RQ:Howell German Diet|chapter=Against Spain|page=26|passage=VVhen I '''deſcend''' into my ſelf, and contemplat my moſt terrible horrible terribility, I can hardly hold my ſelf vvithin my ſelf; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|lines=111–113|page=34|passage=[He] vvith holieſt Meditations fed, / Into himſelf '''deſcended''', and at once / All his great vvork to come before him ſet; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Burke Regicide Peace|letter=3|page=89|passage=Let us '''deſcend''' into our ovvn boſoms; let us aſk ourſelves vvhat are our duties, and vvhat are our means of diſcharging them.}}
### {{lb|en|mathematics}} Of a [[sequence#Noun|sequence]] or [[series]]: to proceed from higher to lower [[value#Noun|values]].
###* {{RQ:Cheyne Philosophical Principles|part=II|subchapter=Corollary I|page=162|passage=''Indefinite'' numbers I ſuppoſe to be intermediate Numbers lying betvveen finite and infinite: For as vve do not '''deſcend''' from 1 to 0 at one Step, but muſt paſs through an infinite Series of Fractions, ½, ⅓, ¼, {{frac|1|5}}, &c.}}
